ply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m\n\n⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]\n\nded constructive. MN Trading Capital founder Michael van de Poppe said in an X post on Friday that the legislation is “the biggest, and historical, bill for the entire industry and can be a strong trigger for the upcoming bull market.” White House crypto advisor Patrick Witt also posted that the CLARITY Act vote was “a major step forward,” but not yet final. Witt said there is still more work to be done before the legislation is ready for prime time and that the administration would keep working in good faith to build the support needed to pass the bill on the Senate floor. Santiment added that any movement toward passage “can and should be considered bullish for crypto (in the long run) because it could finally give the industry clearer rules in the United States.” The piece also noted that if the CLARITY Act passes, more institutional money and powerful players would be expected to enter or re-enter the markets. At the same time, Santiment warned that the largest cryptocurrencies could already have much of the optimism priced in before the bill is officially passed. The broader mood was still cautious: the Crypto Fear & Greed Index posted a Fear score of 31 on Saturday.\\n\\nDecrypt’s roundup echoed the same regulatory catalyst from a market-angle perspective. It described the event as the Clarity Act passing its first major vote in a bipartisan checkpoint, placing the bill at the center of the day’s crypto narrative. The roundup tied the vote to wider market reactions, including renewed optimism across Bitcoin-related assets and attention on how Congress might shape crypto market structure. It presented the vote as an important but incomplete step, not a final legislative win. The theme across both sources was that the market is responding not to enactment, but to momentum: a key committee or procedural checkpoint, rising expectations of clearer rules, and the possibility that institutional capital could respond to improved regulatory certainty. This makes the CLARITY Act story more than a political headline; it is a market structure story with direct implications for Bitcoin, exchanges, trading firms, ETFs, and institutional participation.\\n\\nThe combined picture from the feeds is that crypto traders are treating the CLARITY Act as a major directional signal. On one hand, the immediate price response in Bitcoin was positive, and social sentiment surged. On the other hand, the sentiment data itself looked crowded, which is exactly why Santiment cautioned that enthusiasm can become a contrarian risk. That tension is important: the rally is being powered by expectations of clearer rules, but the same optimism could leave the market vulnerable if legislative progress slows or if the bill stalls before final passage. The policy backdrop is also important because it helps explain why Bitcoin and the rest of the market have been so reactive to congressional movement. The possibility of clearer U.S. rules could reduce uncertainty for institutions, exchanges, and funds that have been waiting for firmer guidance. In that sense, the CLARITY Act is functioning as both a short-term sentiment driver and a potential long-term structural catalyst.\\n\\nThe most specific facts surfaced in the coverage are the Bitcoin spot level of $79,084, the 3.15% rise since May 1, the 1.55-to-1 bullish-versus-bearish comment ratio cited by Santiment, Witt’s insistence that the bill is not finalized, and the Fear & Greed Index reading of 31. Those details anchor the story in current market conditions rather than vague speculation. The central takeaway is that the CLARITY Act has become the dominant policy headline in crypto for the day, with multiple outlets linking the vote to Bitcoin’s price, market sentiment, and expectations for institutional adoption.\",\r\n  \"aggregated_raw_content_note\": \"This summary combines the article text and feed excerpts from Cointelegraph and Decrypt on 2026-05-16, both published within the last 24 hours.\"\r\n}\r\nCHART_COIN: bitcoin[... 23854 more characters truncated]"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":55401,"aggregated":"[1m[38;5;209m🦞 OpenClaw[39m[22m [38;5;216m2026.4.23[39m [38;5;138m(a979721)[39m [38;5;138m—[39m [38;5;167mI've seen your commit messages. We'll work on that together.[39m\r\n\r\n[?25l[90m│[39m\r\n[35m◒[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◑[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◒[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◑[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m[1G[J[35m◒[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◑[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◒[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◑[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m.[1G[J[35m◒[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◑[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◒[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◑[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m..[1G[J[35m◒[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m...[1G[J[35m◐[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent reply…[39m...[1G[J[35m◓[39m  [38;5;209mWaiting for agent 